   "15:24": [
    {
     "text": "**Forgetting increases with longer intervals after encoding**.",
     "children": [
      "The more time that passes after an event is stored, the less of it is remembered.",
      "**Forgetting is not an “all-or-nothing” process**, because part of a memory can be lost while another part remains."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Two experiences of memory show this.",
     "children": [
      "**Familiarity** is associated with **semantic memory**, because the person knows something without recalling the circumstances in which it was learned.",
      "**Recollection** is associated with **episodic memory**, because the person recalls the details of the original experience."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The **remember/know procedure** measures the two.",
     "children": [
      "A participant responds “remember” when the stimulus is familiar and the circumstances of meeting it can also be recalled.",
      "The participant responds “know” when the stimulus is familiar but those circumstances cannot be recalled, and “don’t know” when the stimulus is not remembered at all.",
      "This procedure reveals the **semanticization of remote memories**, which is the **loss of episodic details for memories of long-ago events**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "These ideas matter because forgetting is not all-or-nothing. The details of an event can be lost while the knowledge that it happened remains.",
     "children": [
      "The remember/know procedure measures which part is still there."
     ]
    }
   ],
