   "16:3": [
    {
     "text": "**Encoding** is **acquiring information and transforming it into memory**.",
     "children": [
      "**Retrieval** is **transferring information from LTM to working memory**.",
      "Encoding puts information into long-term memory, and retrieval brings it back out so that it can be used."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "**Maintenance rehearsal** is the **repetition of stimuli that maintains information but does not transfer it to LTM**.",
     "children": [
      "Repeating a phone number over and over keeps it available for the moment.",
      "Once the repetition stops, the number is usually lost, because repetition alone does not store it."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "**Elaborative rehearsal** is **using meanings and connections to help transfer information to LTM**.",
     "children": [
      "The person thinks about what the information means and links it to other things they know.",
      "This kind of rehearsal is what moves information into long-term memory."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The two kinds of rehearsal matter because only one of them stores information. Repeating keeps information available for the moment, and using meaning moves it into long-term memory.",
     "children": [
      "How information is rehearsed decides whether it is remembered later."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "16:4": [
    {
     "text": "**Levels of processing theory** states that **memory depends on how information is encoded**.",
     "children": [
      "What matters is the **depth of processing** the information receives, and not simply how long it is studied."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "**Shallow processing** involves **little attention to meaning** and a **focus on physical features**, such as what a word looks like.",
     "children": [
      "Shallow processing leads to **poor memory**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "**Deep processing** involves **close attention to meaning**.",
     "children": [
      "The person thinks about what the item means and how it relates to other things.",
      "Deep processing leads to **better memory**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Levels of processing matters because it ties memory to the kind of thinking done at encoding. Attention to meaning gives better memory than attention to physical features.",
     "children": [
      "The same word can be remembered well or poorly, depending on how it was processed."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "16:5": [
    {
     "text": "In **Craik and Tulving’s (1975) experiment**, participants answered a question about each word.",
     "children": [
      "The question controlled how deeply the word was processed."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "**Shallow processing (structural or visual)** **focused on physical appearance**, with a question such as **“Is the word in capital letters?”** for the word TABLE. **Intermediate processing (phonemic or acoustic)** **focused on sound**, with a question such as **“Does the word rhyme with weight?”** for the word crate.",
     "children": []
    },
    {
     "text": "**Deep processing (semantic or meaning)** **focused on meaning and context using a fill-in-the-blank sentence**, such as **“Does the word fit in the sentence: ‘He met a ______ in the street’?”** for the word FRIEND. To answer, the participant has to think about what the word means.",
     "children": []
    },
    {
     "text": "The three questions matter because they control the depth of processing. The same kind of word is processed by its appearance, by its sound or by its meaning.",
     "children": [
      "The question is what changes, so the question sets the depth."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "16:6": [
    {
     "text": "Each trial in **Craik and Tulving (1975)** had three steps.",
     "children": [
      "First a **question was asked**, for example whether the word is in capital letters.",
      "Then a **word was presented**, for example bird.",
      "Then the participant **answered the question**, for example “No.”"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Memory for the words was tested afterward, and the **percent correct** depended on the kind of question.",
     "children": [
      "It was lowest for the **capital letters** question, at about 15 percent.",
      "It was higher for the **rhyme** question, at about 48 percent.",
      "It was highest for the **fill in the blanks** question, at about 80 percent."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Deeper processing therefore produced better memory.",
     "children": [
      "The words were the same kind of words in every condition, and only the depth of the question changed.",
      "This is the result that levels of processing theory predicts."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The result matters because memory rose step by step with depth. The capital letters question gave the least memory and the sentence question gave the most.",
     "children": [
      "This is the evidence for levels of processing theory."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "16:7": [
    {
     "text": "Several other factors aid encoding.",
     "children": [
      "They are **visual imagery**, the **self-reference effect**, the **generation effect**, **organizing to-be-remembered information**, **relating words to survival value**, and **retrieval practice**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Visual imagery means forming a mental image that connects the items to be remembered.",
     "children": [
      "The self-reference effect is better memory for information that is related to oneself.",
      "The generation effect is better memory for material a person generates than for material the person only reads.",
      "Visual imagery, the self-reference effect and the generation effect each add meaning or connections at encoding."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "A comparison of two groups shows the effect of visual imagery on word pairs such as boat and tree.",
     "children": [
      "A **repetition group** repeated the pair, and an **imagery group** formed an image that combined the two items, such as a boat in a tree.",
      "The **percent correct recall** was more than twice as high for the imagery group as for the repetition group."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "These factors matter because visual imagery, the self-reference effect and the generation effect add meaning or connections at encoding. Forming an image of two items together links them, and repeating them does not.",
     "children": [
      "The imagery group recalled more than twice as many pairs as the repetition group."
     ]
    }
   ],
