   "15:36": [
    {
     "text": "**Perfect and Askew (1994)** studied priming in everyday experience.",
     "children": [
      "The **propaganda effect** is that people are **more likely to rate statements read or heard before as being true**.",
      "Simply having met a statement earlier makes it seem more believable later."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The propaganda effect **involves implicit memory because it can occur when people are not aware of previously seeing or hearing the statement**.",
     "children": [
      "The earlier exposure changes the later judgment without any conscious memory of the exposure."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "This has **implications for advertisements**.",
     "children": [
      "An advertisement that a person has seen before can be rated more favorably later, even when the person does not remember having seen it."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The propaganda effect matters because belief can change without awareness. A statement seems more true simply because it was met before.",
     "children": [
      "This is why it is treated as implicit memory."
     ]
    }
   ],
