   "15:39": [
    {
     "text": "**Classical conditioning** involves **pairing a neutral stimulus with a reflexive response**.",
     "children": [
      "After the pairing, the neutral stimulus alone produces the response."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Classical conditioning **involves implicit memory when the person has forgotten about the original pairing of the stimulus and the response**.",
     "children": [
      "The response still occurs, but the person has no conscious memory of how it was learned."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Two everyday examples show this.",
     "children": [
      "In the **fear of dogs after a childhood bite**, **an adult feels an automatic spike in heart rate and anxiety around large dogs, even though they were bitten as a toddler and have no conscious memory of the incident**.",
      "In a **song triggering sudden sadness or nostalgia**, **hearing an old song from a stressful period in life causes a sudden tight feeling in the chest before the person consciously remembers why**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Conditioning matters here because the response outlasts the memory of its cause. The reaction still occurs after the original pairing is forgotten.",
     "children": [
      "That makes it implicit memory."
     ]
    }
   ],
