   "16:15": [
    {
     "text": "The first principle of effective studying is to **elaborate**, which means to **associate what you are learning to what you already know**.",
     "children": [
      "The second is to **generate questions and self-test**.",
      "Self-testing is retrieval practice, and it improves later memory."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The third principle is to **take breaks**.",
     "children": [
      "The **spacing effect** is that **memory is better for multiple short study sessions** than for one long session.",
      "In addition, **consolidation is enhanced by sleep after studying**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The fourth principle is to **avoid the “illusion of learning.”** **Familiarity does not mean comprehension**.",
     "children": [
      "Rereading makes material feel familiar, and that feeling can be mistaken for understanding it."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "These principles matter because each one comes from a memory finding. Elaborating uses meaning, self-testing is retrieval practice, and breaks use the spacing effect.",
     "children": [
      "Feeling familiar with material is not the same as understanding it."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "16:16": [],
   "16:17": [
    {
     "text": "**Retrieval** is the **process of transferring information from LTM back into working memory (consciousness)**.",
     "children": [
      "Information that is stored in long-term memory is not in awareness until it is retrieved."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "**Most of our failures of memory are failures to retrieve**.",
     "children": [
      "The information is often still stored, but the person cannot bring it back at that moment.",
      "This is why the conditions at retrieval, such as the cues that are available, matter so much for memory."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Retrieval matters because stored information is useful only when it can be brought back. Most failures of memory are failures to retrieve.",
     "children": [
      "The information is often still stored."
     ]
    }
   ],
