   "16:33": [
    {
     "text": "The **standard model of consolidation** describes systems consolidation.",
     "children": [
      "According to this model, **after encoding, the activity of the hippocampus fades with time**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "At first, the hippocampus is connected to the areas of the cortex that were active during the experience, and it links them together.",
     "children": [
      "Over time, the connections among the cortical areas themselves become stronger, and the connections with the hippocampus become weaker."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "In the end, the cortical areas are connected directly to one another, and the hippocampus is no longer needed for that memory.",
     "children": [
      "In the standard model, the hippocampus is essential for recent memories and is not needed for remote memories."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The standard model matters because it gives the hippocampus a temporary role. The hippocampus links the cortical areas at first, and its activity fades with time.",
     "children": [
      "In the end the cortical areas are connected directly."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "16:34": [
    {
     "text": "The **standard model of consolidation is based partly on observation of injury or trauma-related memory loss**.",
     "children": [
      "What people forget after an injury gives evidence about how memories are consolidated."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "**Retrograde amnesia** is the **loss of memory for events prior to the trauma**.",
     "children": [
      "The person cannot remember things that happened before the injury."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "**Graded amnesia** means that **memory for recent events is more fragile than for remote events**.",
     "children": [
      "The amnesia is worst for events just before the injury and becomes less severe for earlier events.",
      "This fits the standard model, because recent memories have had less time to be consolidated."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Graded amnesia matters because it fits the standard model. Recent memories are more fragile than remote memories.",
     "children": [
      "Recent memories have had less time to be consolidated."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "16:35": [
    {
     "text": "Memory loss after an injury is described by its direction in time.",
     "children": [
      "**Retrograde** amnesia concerns the **past**, which is the period before the injury.",
      "**Anterograde** amnesia concerns the **future**, which is the period after the injury."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "**Graded amnesia** affects the period just before the **injury** most strongly.",
     "children": [
      "The loss becomes smaller for events further back in time, and **remote memory**, which is memory for events from long ago, is the least affected."
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The timeline matters because it separates two directions of memory loss. Retrograde is for the past before the injury, and anterograde is for the future after it.",
     "children": [
      "Graded amnesia is strongest just before the injury."
     ]
    }
   ],
